MODULES.push({
id:'m12',title:'Taxes, rules and running it like a business',blurb:'Short-term gains, wash sales, trader tax status, futures and crypto tax notes, records, and getting professional help.',
goal:'Know the tax and record-keeping realities before your first profit, so a good year does not become a surprise bill.',
outcomes:['Explain how short-term gains, losses and estimated taxes work for U.S. day traders','Calculate a wash sale adjustment','Describe trader tax status and the mark-to-market election, and why most beginners should not rely on it','Summarize tax treatment differences for futures and crypto','Set up records and know when to hire a professional'],
lessons:[
{t:'U.S. tax basics for day traders',m:11,h:`
<div class="callout warn"><strong class="t">Scope</strong><p>This module describes general U.S. federal rules. Other countries differ, and state rules vary. It is education, not tax advice. Rules change, so confirm with a qualified tax professional, ideally one who works with active traders.</p></div>
<h3>Short-term gains</h3>
<p>Profits on assets held for one year or less are short-term capital gains, taxed at your ordinary income rate. Day trades are always short-term. Long-term rates, which are lower, apply only after holding more than a year.</p>
<h3>Losses</h3>
<p>Capital losses first offset capital gains. If losses exceed gains, up to $3,000 a year (for individuals) can offset other income, and the rest carries forward to future years.</p>
<h3>Taxes on gains you gave back</h3>
<p>Tax is due on <b>realized</b> gains for the year. If you make $20,000 by June and give back $15,000 by December, you owe tax only on the $5,000 net for the year. But a big gain in one year followed by a loss in the next does not refund the earlier tax, because an individual's capital losses generally carry forward, not back.</p>
<div class="example"><strong class="t">Example: after-tax result (illustrative)</strong><p>You net $20,000 in short-term gains for the year. Suppose your combined federal and state marginal rate is 29%. Tax = $5,800; after-tax profit = $14,200. Add data fees and platform costs (say $1,200), and the real result is closer to $13,000.</p></div>
<h3>Forms and payments</h3>
<ul>
<li>Your broker sends <b>Form 1099-B</b> summarizing sales. Trades are reported on <b>Form 8949</b> and totaled on <b>Schedule D</b>.</li>
<li>If you owe tax that is not withheld, you generally must pay <b>quarterly estimated taxes</b> to avoid underpayment penalties.</li>
<li>Higher earners may owe the 3.8% <b>net investment income tax</b> on top.</li>
<li>Set aside a percentage of every profit as you go into a separate account.</li>
</ul>`},
{t:'The wash sale rule',m:8,h:`
<p>If you sell a security at a loss and buy the same or a "substantially identical" security within 30 days before or after the sale (a 61-day window), the loss is disallowed for that tax year. Instead, the disallowed loss is added to the cost basis of the replacement shares, so you recover it later.</p>
<div class="example"><strong class="t">Worked example</strong>
<p>You buy 100 shares of XYZ at $50 and sell at $48. Loss: $200. Two weeks later you buy 100 shares at $47.</p>
<ul><li>The $200 loss is disallowed under the wash sale rule.</li>
<li>New cost basis = $47 purchase price + $2 per share disallowed loss = <b>$49 per share</b> ($4,900 total).</li>
<li>Later you sell at $52: proceeds $5,200 minus basis $4,900 = <b>$300 gain</b> (rather than $500 if the loss had been allowed at the time).</li></ul></div>
<h3>Why it matters to active traders</h3>
<p>If you trade the same few stocks all year, wash sales can pile up, especially around December 31. You may owe tax on gains while a large part of your losses is deferred. Brokers track wash sales within one account, but not across accounts or across your IRA, so your true position may differ from the 1099-B.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong class="t">Practical steps</strong><p>Use your broker's wash sale report. Watch year-end trading in stocks where you hold losses. Do not buy in an IRA the same stock you sold at a loss in a taxable account without checking the rules with a professional.</p></div>`},
{t:'Trader tax status and mark-to-market',m:10,h:`
<p>The IRS recognizes three categories: <b>investors</b>, <b>dealers</b> and <b>traders</b>. Most people who buy and sell securities are investors for tax purposes, even if they trade often.</p>
<h3>Trader tax status: the three tests</h3>
<p>According to IRS guidance, to be a trader in securities, you must:</p>
<ol>
<li>Seek to profit from daily market movements in the prices of securities, not from dividends, interest or long-term appreciation.</li>
<li>Have activity that is substantial.</li>
<li>Carry it out with continuity and regularity.</li>
</ol>
<p>The IRS looks at your holding periods, frequency of trades, dollar amounts, how much of your income depends on trading, and the time you devote to it. Meeting all three is a high bar, and one that is often contested in audits.</p>
<h3>The mark-to-market election (Section 475(f))</h3>
<p>A qualifying trader can elect mark-to-market accounting. Under it:</p>
<ul>
<li>Gains and losses are treated as ordinary (reported on Form 4797), so the $3,000 capital loss cap does not apply.</li>
<li>Wash sale rules do not apply to securities under the election.</li>
<li>Open positions are treated as sold at year-end (marked to market).</li>
</ul>
<p>The election has a strict deadline: for an existing taxpayer, it generally must be made by the unextended due date of the prior year's tax return. Late elections are generally not allowed, and revocation involves formal filings. The election also means all gains are ordinary income, with no long-term rate for securities held over a year, and it can be a poor fit for someone who also invests.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong class="t">For beginners</strong><p>Most beginners will not qualify, and many will not still be trading a year later. Do not try to elect anything without a tax professional who works with traders. Learn the rules, keep good records, and decide after you have real evidence you are trading substantially and regularly.</p></div>`},
{t:'Futures, options and crypto: different tax treatment',m:8,h:`
<h3>Futures (Section 1256 contracts)</h3>
<p>In the U.S., regulated futures contracts generally receive special treatment: gains and losses are marked to market at year-end and taxed 60% as long-term and 40% as short-term, regardless of how long you held. This can produce a lower blended rate than ordinary short-term gains. The wash sale rule does not apply to these contracts. Reported on Form 6781. Some broad-based index options are also treated under Section 1256.</p>
<h3>Options on stocks</h3>
<p>Options on individual stocks are generally taxed like stock trades (short-term if held a year or less). Wash sale rules can apply between options and the underlying stock.</p>
<h3>Crypto</h3>
<p>The IRS treats digital assets as property, so each sale or trade is a taxable event with a gain or loss to report. The wash sale rule has applied to securities, and its treatment of crypto has been debated and subject to change, so check current rules. Keep an exact record of every transaction across every exchange and wallet.</p>
<h3>Forex</h3>
<p>Spot forex may be taxed as ordinary gains and losses by default under Section 988, with elections available to treat it under other rules. This is complex; get advice.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong class="t">Do not choose an instrument for the tax break</strong><p>A tax advantage is worth nothing if the instrument makes you lose more. Tax should be a check on your plan, not the reason for it.</p></div>`},
{t:'Records, structure and professional help',m:7,h:`
<h3>Records to keep</h3>
<ul>
<li>Broker statements and 1099-B forms every year.</li>
<li>Your journal (trade-level detail supports any claim of trading activity).</li>
<li>Receipts for data, software, education and equipment (whether they are deductible depends on your status, so ask).</li>
<li>Records of deposits and withdrawals to separate trading capital from personal money.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Structure</h3>
<p>Many beginners trade in a personal taxable account. Some later use an LLC or S-corporation, which brings costs and rules of its own and generally does not by itself change how securities gains are taxed. Discuss with a professional before forming anything.</p>
<h3>Retirement accounts</h3>
<p>Trading in an IRA can defer taxes but excludes margin and complicates loss deductions, since losses inside an IRA cannot offset other income. Rule 2270 also warns against using retirement savings for day trading.</p>
<h3>Hiring help</h3>
<p>A CPA or enrolled agent with active-trader clients is worth the fee once you trade with real money. Bring your 1099-B, wash sale report, and journal.</p>
<h3>Outside the U.S.</h3>
<p>Rules differ widely: some countries tax trading gains as income, some as capital gains, some have allowances, and some restrict retail leverage more tightly. Check the rules of your country of residence before opening an account.</p>`}
],
quiz:[
{q:'Day trading profits in the U.S. are generally taxed as:',o:['Long-term capital gains','Short-term capital gains at ordinary income rates','Tax-free','Only at state level'],a:1,e:'Holding a year or less produces short-term gains taxed at ordinary rates.'},
{q:'Up to how much net capital loss can an individual deduct against other income per year?',o:['$300','$3,000','$30,000','Unlimited'],a:1,e:'The limit is $3,000, with the rest carried forward.'},
{q:'Buy at $50, sell at $48 (loss $2 per share), rebuy within 30 days at $47. New basis per share?',o:['$47','$48','$49','$50'],a:2,e:'$47 plus the disallowed $2 loss per share is $49.'},
{q:'Which is NOT one of the IRS trader tax status tests?',o:['Seeking to profit from daily price movements','Activity is substantial','Continuity and regularity','Owning at least $25,000'],a:3,e:'There is no minimum account balance test.'},
{q:'The mark-to-market election is best described as:',o:['Something you can elect any time before filing after year-end','Available only to qualifying traders with a strict deadline, and it changes how gains and losses are treated','Automatic after 100 trades','Required for all day traders'],a:1,e:'It has a strict deadline and consequences, and only traders may elect it.'},
{q:'Section 1256 futures gains are generally treated as:',o:['100% short-term','60% long-term and 40% short-term','100% long-term','Tax-exempt'],a:1,e:'The 60/40 rule applies regardless of holding period.'},
{q:'Why should you set aside tax money from profits as you go?',o:['To avoid a surprise bill and underpayment penalties','It is required by your broker','It reduces your tax rate','Banks require it'],a:0,e:'Tax is due on realized gains, and estimated payments are often needed.'}
],
work:{title:'Set up your tax and records system',intro:'Handle the boring things before there are profits to protect.',tasks:['Find a CPA or enrolled agent who works with active traders and note their fee for a consultation.','Create a folder and naming scheme for statements, 1099-B forms, journals and receipts.','Read the IRS Topic 429 page (linked in Sources) and write down the three trader tests in your own words.','Work through the wash sale example yourself with different numbers.','Estimate your after-tax return on a hypothetical $10,000 profit at your own tax rate.','Open a separate savings account and decide what percent of each profit you will move into it.'],deliver:'A one-page tax and records plan and your after-tax estimate.'}
});

MODULES.push({
id:'m13',title:'Scams, gurus and ethics',blurb:'The education economy, red flags, verification tools, common frauds, and the legal and ethical lines for anyone who teaches.',
goal:'Protect your money from bad actors and, if you ever share ideas or teach, do it in a way that is honest and lawful.',
outcomes:['Explain how the trading education economy makes money','Apply a due-diligence checklist and use free verification tools','Recognize pump-and-dump, signal-group, impersonation and crypto scams','Describe the legal boundaries around education, advice and earnings claims','Write honest results and risk disclosures'],
lessons:[
{t:'The trading education economy',m:9,h:`
<p>Day trading looks glamorous. That attracts a large industry of courses, signal groups, "mentorships", software subscriptions and "funded account" programs. Some offer real value. Many make more money selling to beginners than they ever made trading, because selling education has better margins than trading and does not require a market edge.</p>
<h3>Why the model works</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Survivorship and selection.</b> Sellers show their best days and the students who happened to do well. The rest are invisible.</li>
<li><b>Lifestyle marketing.</b> Cars, watches, rented houses and travel photos create desire. They prove that someone has money, not that trading paid for it.</li>
<li><b>Recurring revenue.</b> A cheap course leads to expensive upsells, signals and mentorships. The seller's income depends on sales volume, not on your results.</li>
<li><b>Urgency.</b> Countdown timers and limited spots reduce the time you take to research.</li>
<li><b>Evaluation firms.</b> Many "funded account" programs charge fees for evaluations that most applicants fail, and may earn most of their revenue from those fees.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Rule 2270's warning</h3>
<p>FINRA's day-trading risk disclosure tells customers to be wary of advertisements that emphasize the potential for large profits. That guidance applies equally to the marketing of education products.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong class="t">Not all education is bad</strong><p>Good educators teach risk management first, admit the odds, show verifiable results including losses, and do not need you to buy today. Use those as your test.</p></div>`},
{t:'Red flags and how to verify claims',m:10,h:`
<h3>Red flags</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Guaranteed or unrealistic returns:</b> "$1,000 a day", "10x in 30 days." No honest trader guarantees returns.</li>
<li><b>Screenshots as proof.</b> Screenshots and demo accounts are trivial to fake or cherry-pick.</li>
<li><b>No verifiable track record.</b> Real evidence is broker statements, ideally over several years including losing periods, or third-party verification.</li>
<li><b>Pressure tactics.</b></li>
<li><b>Hidden costs.</b> A cheap entry product feeding expensive tiers.</li>
<li><b>Signal services</b> where you are told when to buy just as the seller may be selling.</li>
<li><b>Requests for your login,</b> or to send money or crypto to an individual: always a scam.</li>
<li><b>Refund refusal or vague terms.</b></li>
</ul>
<h3>Questions before paying</h3>
<ol>
<li>Can they show audited or broker-verified results over years, including drawdowns?</li>
<li>Do they say clearly that most day traders lose money and share what results their own students typically get (not testimonials, but data)?</li>
<li>What is the total cost with all upsells? What is the refund policy?</li>
<li>Do they teach risk management and psychology, or only entries?</li>
<li>Are they registered if they give personalized advice?</li>
<li>Are there independent reviews, complaints or regulatory actions?</li>
</ol>
<h3>Free verification tools</h3>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it checks</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>FINRA BrokerCheck</td><td>Brokers and broker-dealers: registrations, disclosures, complaints</td></tr>
<tr><td>SEC Investment Adviser Public Disclosure (IAPD)</td><td>Registered investment advisers</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFA BASIC</td><td>Futures, forex and commodity firms and individuals</td></tr>
<tr><td>SEC EDGAR and litigation releases</td><td>Company filings and enforcement actions</td></tr>
<tr><td>State securities regulator</td><td>State registrations and complaints</td></tr>
<tr><td>FTC and BBB complaint records</td><td>Consumer complaints about sellers</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`},
{t:'Common frauds and protecting your accounts',m:10,h:`
<h3>Pump and dump</h3>
<p>Promoters accumulate a thinly traded stock, hype it on social media or in chat groups, and sell into the buying they create. Late buyers are left with losses. The SEC has charged many schemes that used social media investment clubs.</p>
<h3>Signal and copy-trading groups</h3>
<p>By the time you see a signal, the price has often moved, and the operator may be trading against your entry. Any paid group whose profits depend on people following its calls has a conflict of interest.</p>
<h3>Fake platforms and crypto romance scams</h3>
<p>A stranger builds trust over weeks, then steers you to a fake trading site that displays fake profits. When you try to withdraw, you are asked for "taxes" or "fees." Regulators warn these are among the costliest scams. Once crypto is sent, recovery is almost impossible.</p>
<h3>Impersonation and recovery scams</h3>
<p>Fraudsters pose as your broker, "support," or regulators, and later contact scam victims offering to recover lost funds for a fee. Real regulators never charge to recover money.</p>
<h3>Protect your accounts</h3>
<ul>
<li>Unique passwords in a password manager and app-based two-factor authentication.</li>
<li>Never give login credentials, one-time codes or remote access to anyone.</li>
<li>Type your broker's address yourself; do not click links in texts or emails.</li>
<li>Set alerts for logins, withdrawals and large orders.</li>
<li>Withdraw to accounts in your own name only.</li>
</ul>
<h3>If you are targeted or scammed</h3>
<p>Stop sending money. Preserve records. Report to the SEC (sec.gov/tcr), the FTC (reportfraud.ftc.gov), FINRA, the CFTC for futures and forex, your state regulator and your bank or card issuer immediately.</p>`},
{t:'If you teach or share ideas: legal lines',m:12,h:`
<div class="callout warn"><strong class="t">Not legal advice</strong><p>This lesson explains the kinds of rules that apply to people who teach trading, share trade ideas or sell signals. The details depend on the facts and your jurisdiction. Talk to a securities and advertising lawyer before you sell anything.</p></div>
<h3>Education versus advice</h3>
<p>In the U.S., the Investment Advisers Act regulates people who, for compensation, advise others about securities. General educational publications may fall within a "publisher's exclusion" recognized by the Supreme Court in a case called <i>Lowe v. SEC</i>, but personalized advice, specific buy and sell recommendations to individuals, or paid signals can cross into regulated activity. For futures, commodity trading advisor (CTA) rules can apply, with exemptions in some cases. Do not assume that a disclaimer saying "not financial advice" removes regulatory obligations.</p>
<h3>Truthful advertising</h3>
<p>The Federal Trade Commission Act prohibits deceptive claims, including in earnings and results marketing. The FTC has proposed stronger rules on earnings claims and business opportunity sellers, and it enforces existing rules against misleading income claims and fake testimonials. Practically:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Do not imply typical results are your results.</b> If you show great results, you must be able to substantiate them, and if they are not typical, say what typical results are.</li>
<li><b>Testimonials must be real</b> and reflect typical experience, or you must clearly disclose otherwise. Paid or incentivized endorsements must be disclosed (the FTC's Endorsement Guides).</li>
<li><b>Do not present lifestyle as proof of trading profits</b> when it is not, or when the source of the money is course sales.</li>
<li><b>Disclose material connections:</b> affiliate links, broker commissions, prop firm referral fees.</li>
<li><b>Honor your refund promises.</b></li>
</ul>
<h3>Fraud statutes</h3>
<p>The SEC and CFTC pursue people who make false statements to induce trading, including on social media. Fabricated or altered screenshots, invented track records or promoting stocks you hold without disclosure can lead to enforcement.</p>`},
{t:'Building a course that is honest and durable',m:8,h:`
<p>There is a defensible, lasting way to run a trading education business. It is less flashy than the guru model, and it lasts longer.</p>
<h3>Principles</h3>
<ol>
<li><b>Lead with the odds.</b> Show the studies. It builds credibility with skeptical people, who are the customers who stay.</li>
<li><b>Teach risk first.</b> Most of your content should be about not losing, not about winning.</li>
<li><b>Show verifiable results, warts included.</b> Broker statements or third-party verification, covering losing months, in real accounts, in real time.</li>
<li><b>Separate results from lifestyle.</b> If your income comes from education, say so. Never let followers assume it is trading profit.</li>
<li><b>Specific disclosures.</b> A short, clear statement near every claim.</li>
<li><b>Refund and complaint policies</b> that you honor.</li>
<li><b>Student outcome tracking.</b> Collect anonymized data on how students do, and publish it.</li>
<li><b>No signals; no personalized advice</b> unless properly registered.</li>
<li><b>No pressure.</b> Let people decide slowly.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Sample plain-language disclosure (adapt with counsel)</h3>
<div class="callout"><p>Most people who day trade lose money. Studies of retail day traders across several countries have found that a large majority lose and that only a small percentage are consistently profitable after costs. This course is educational only. It is not financial, tax or legal advice. Results shown are examples; they are not typical and do not guarantee similar results. My income comes from selling education, not solely from trading. You can lose all the money you trade.</p></div>
<h3>Why honesty is a business advantage</h3>
<p>Regulatory scrutiny of earnings claims is increasing, and audiences have grown wary of lifestyle marketing. A brand that says "most of you will lose, and here is how to lose less and find out cheaply" is rare, believable and harder to attack.</p>`}
],
quiz:[
{q:'Why do many trading educators earn more from selling education than from trading?',o:['Trading is illegal','Education has better margins and does not require a market edge','Brokers pay them','Courses are always free'],a:1,e:'Education sales scale without the need for consistent trading profits.'},
{q:'Which is the most reliable evidence of a trader\'s results?',o:['Screenshots','A demo account','Broker-verified statements over years including losing periods','Testimonials'],a:2,e:'Screenshots and demos are easy to fake or cherry-pick.'},
{q:'Someone you met online steers you to a "trading platform" that shows big profits but demands "taxes" to withdraw. This is most likely:',o:['A tax rule','A scam','A bank error','A margin call'],a:1,e:'Fake profits plus fees to withdraw is a hallmark of the crypto romance/pig-butchering type scam.'},
{q:'Which tool checks registered investment advisers?',o:['FINRA BrokerCheck only','SEC Investment Adviser Public Disclosure (IAPD)','Social media followers','A CPA'],a:1,e:'IAPD covers registered advisers; BrokerCheck covers brokers and broker-dealers.'},
{q:'Does adding "not financial advice" to a paid signal service remove regulatory obligations?',o:['Yes always','No. Paid, personalized recommendations may still be regulated','Only for stocks','Only for crypto'],a:1,e:'What you actually do matters more than the label.'},
{q:'A compliant approach to showing student results is to:',o:['Show only the best student','Show typical results and clearly disclose that most do not achieve such results','Never mention results','Use fake names'],a:1,e:'Claims must be substantiated and not misleading about typical results.'},
{q:'A "recovery service" contacts you after a scam and asks for an upfront fee. This is:',o:['Standard practice','Another scam','A regulatory requirement','A tax refund'],a:1,e:'Regulators do not charge to recover funds; recovery scams target prior victims.'}
],
work:{title:'Due diligence and a disclosure statement',intro:'Practice evaluating a seller, then write the honest disclosure you would want to read.',tasks:['Pick a well-known trading educator or "funded account" firm. Apply the six due-diligence questions and record what you can and cannot verify.','Look up two brokers on BrokerCheck and one futures or forex firm on NFA BASIC.','Find one SEC or FTC enforcement action involving a trading-related scheme and summarize how it worked.','Write your own plain-language risk and results disclosure of at least 100 words.','List five specific claims that you will never make in any marketing.','Write your own security rules: what you will never share and never click.'],deliver:'Your due-diligence write-up, the enforcement summary, and your disclosure statement.'}
});

MODULES.push({
id:'m14',title:'Capstone: your plan and your 12 months',blurb:'The 12-month roadmap, a trading plan you can copy out, ongoing study, and the decision framework: continue, scale, switch or stop.',
goal:'Pull everything together into a written plan and a schedule, with rules for when to continue, scale up, change course or stop.',
outcomes:['Follow a 12-month roadmap with exit criteria for each phase','Write a complete trading plan','Choose a study plan from credible sources','Apply a decision framework at the 12-month review'],
lessons:[
{t:'The 12-month roadmap',m:9,h:`
<p>Real skill takes time. Treat the dates as approximate and do not advance until you have met the exit criteria of the current phase.</p>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Focus</th><th>Exit criteria</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Months 1 to 2<br>Foundations</td><td>Modules 0 to 5. Learn terms, charts, orders. Watch live markets without trading. Set up paper account and journal.</td><td>You can explain bid/ask, order types, stops, R, sizing and expectancy from memory. Assignments 0 to 5 done.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Months 3 to 4<br>Playbook and testing</td><td>Modules 6 and 9. One or two setups. Backtest 100+ trades each. Write playbook pages.</td><td>Backtest complete with statistics and confidence range. Pass criteria written beforehand and met.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Months 4 to 6<br>Paper trading</td><td>Modules 7, 8, 10. Forward test in real time with realistic costs and full journaling.</td><td>100+ paper trades, 90%+ rule adherence, positive net expectancy, drawdown within limits.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Months 6 to 9<br>Small live size</td><td>Trade at 0.25% risk per trade. Focus on emotions with real money, not profit.</td><td>50+ live trades, discipline intact, losses within tuition budget.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Months 9 to 11<br>Refine</td><td>Cut losing setups, study leaks, scale in small steps only if data supports it.</td><td>150+ live trades with positive expectancy and controlled drawdown.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Month 12<br>Evaluate</td><td>Honest review: P&amp;L after all costs and estimated taxes, hours spent, stress, sleep.</td><td>A written decision: continue, scale, switch to swing trading or investing, or stop.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>A monthly rhythm</h3>
<ul>
<li>Week 1 to 3: execute and journal.</li>
<li>Last few days of the month: monthly review, playbook updates, and tax set-aside.</li>
<li>Every quarter: re-read your rules card and tuition budget.</li>
</ul>`},
{t:'Your trading plan',m:10,h:`
<p>Fill in each box. It saves in this browser as you type. When you are done, copy it into a document you keep next to your trading screen and update it quarterly.</p>
<div data-w="plan"></div>
<h3>How to use it</h3>
<ul>
<li>Re-read it before every session.</li>
<li>If you want to break a rule, write the reason in your journal first, and wait 24 hours before changing the plan.</li>
<li>Only change the plan on your review dates, not in the middle of a bad week.</li>
</ul>`},
{t:'Ongoing study: sources worth your time',m:8,h:`
<h3>Regulators and official education</h3>
<ul>
<li>SEC Investor.gov: day trading, margin and crypto alerts</li>
<li>FINRA investor insights: intraday margin, options, crypto risk</li>
<li>IRS Topic 429 and the IRS guidance on traders</li>
<li>CFTC customer advisories</li>
</ul>
<h3>Books</h3>
<ul>
<li><i>Trading in the Zone</i>, Mark Douglas (psychology and probabilistic thinking)</li>
<li><i>Trade Your Way to Financial Freedom</i>, Van Tharp (expectancy, R multiples, position sizing)</li>
<li><i>Market Wizards</i>, Jack Schwager (interviews with professional traders)</li>
<li><i>A Random Walk Down Wall Street</i>, Burton Malkiel (why beating markets is hard)</li>
<li><i>Technical Analysis of the Financial Markets</i>, John Murphy (charting reference)</li>
<li><i>Thinking, Fast and Slow</i>, Daniel Kahneman (biases)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Research</h3>
<ul>
<li>Chague, De-Losso and Giovannetti (2020), "Day Trading for a Living?"</li>
<li>Barber, Lee, Liu and Odean (2014), "The Cross-Section of Speculator Skill: Evidence from Day Trading"</li>
<li>Barber and Odean (2000), "Trading Is Hazardous to Your Wealth"</li>
<li>Odean (1998), "Are Investors Reluctant to Realize Their Losses?"</li>
<li>Kahneman and Tversky (1979), "Prospect Theory"</li>
<li>S&amp;P Dow Jones Indices SPIVA scorecards</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><strong class="t">Communities</strong><p>Choose them carefully. Prefer groups that share journals and statistics and discuss risk, over groups that share screenshots of profits and hot tips. Never follow a stranger's trade without doing your own analysis.</p></div>`},
{t:'Continue, scale, switch or stop',m:8,h:`
<p>At month 12, review honestly. Numbers first, feelings second.</p>
<h3>Decision framework</h3>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Evidence</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Positive net expectancy over 150+ live trades, after costs and estimated tax; rule adherence 90%+; drawdown inside limits; you are calm</td><td><b>Continue.</b> Scale risk in small steps (for example from 0.25% to 0.5%) only after further data.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Expectancy near zero or unclear; discipline good</td><td><b>Extend</b> the learning phase at small size for another quarter, or refine the setup.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Negative expectancy after costs; rule breaks are rare</td><td><b>Setup problem.</b> Retire it, go back to testing, or switch to swing trading, which has lower costs.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Negative results mostly from rule breaks and tilt</td><td><b>Behavior problem.</b> Halve size, use hard circuit breakers, or take a break.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tuition budget spent, or signs of compulsion, or significant life stress</td><td><b>Stop.</b> Withdraw, review, and consider alternatives. This is a valid and often wise outcome.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Alternatives that are not failure</h3>
<ul>
<li>Swing trading: days to weeks, lower costs and less screen time.</li>
<li>Long-term investing in diversified low-cost funds, which has strong evidence behind it.</li>
<li>Continuing to trade tiny as a hobby with a fixed, affordable annual budget.</li>
<li>Using your new skills in risk management, analysis and discipline in another career or field.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><strong class="t">Final word</strong><p>Day trading is a difficult, competitive, high-risk activity where most participants lose money. If you try it, do it slowly, with money you can afford to lose, with small size and strict rules, and with a journal. If the data says you have no edge, stopping is the professional decision. Take the final exam from the menu, then finish your plan.</p></div>`}
],
quiz:[
{q:'What risk per trade does the roadmap recommend for the first live phase?',o:['5%','2%','0.25%','10%'],a:2,e:'The first live phase uses tiny size to learn how real money changes behavior.'},
{q:'What do you do if you want to break a plan rule mid-week?',o:['Break it if it feels right','Write the reason in your journal and wait; change the plan only on review dates','Double size to compensate','Delete the plan'],a:1,e:'Changes belong to calm review, not to the heat of the moment.'},
{q:'Negative results mostly from rule breaks and tilt point to:',o:['A setup problem','A behavior problem: halve size, use circuit breakers','A bad broker','Bad luck'],a:1,e:'If the setup works when followed, discipline is the leak.'},
{q:'Which community is preferable?',o:['One that shares profit screenshots and hot tips','One that shares journals and statistics and discusses risk','One that requires your login','One that sells signals'],a:1,e:'Transparent data and risk discussion signal better quality.'},
{q:'At month 12 your tuition budget is spent and results are negative. The framework says:',o:['Add money','Stop, review and consider alternatives','Increase leverage','Trade more often'],a:1,e:'Stopping is a valid, disciplined outcome.'},
{q:'Which is a valid alternative to day trading?',o:['Swing trading or long-term index investing','Trading larger size','Copying a signals group','Using margin'],a:0,e:'Lower-cost approaches can suit many people better.'}
],
work:{title:'Finish your plan and schedule your reviews',intro:'The last assignment turns the course into a personal operating system.',tasks:['Complete every box of the trading plan builder and copy the text into a document.','Schedule your weekly and monthly reviews in your calendar for the next three months.','Write your graduation checklist from Module 10 with today\'s status for each item.','Set a date for your 12-month evaluation and write your decision framework.','Choose one book and one research paper from the study list and set a date to finish them.','Take the final exam and note the topics you missed for review.'],deliver:'Your completed plan document, a calendar of reviews, and your final exam result.'}
});

window.GLOSSARY=[
['Ask','The lowest price at which a seller is currently willing to sell.'],
['ATR (average true range)','An indicator measuring how far a stock typically moves in a period; used to size stops.'],
['Backtest','Applying trading rules to historical data to see how they would have performed.'],
['Bid','The highest price at which a buyer is currently willing to buy.'],
['Bracket order','An entry order with a linked profit target and protective stop; when one exits, the other cancels.'],
['Breakout','Price moving through a support or resistance level.'],
['Broker','The firm that executes your trades and holds your account.'],
['Call option','A contract giving the buyer the right, not the obligation, to buy a security at the strike price by expiration.'],
['Candlestick','A chart element showing open, high, low and close for a period.'],
['Cash account','A brokerage account limited to money you have; no borrowing.'],
['CFTC','Commodity Futures Trading Commission, the U.S. regulator of futures and certain derivatives.'],
['Circuit breaker','A rule pausing trading in the whole market after a large decline (7%, 13% or 20% in the S&P 500).'],
['Correlation','How much two positions tend to move together.'],
['Cover','To buy back shares you sold short.'],
['Delta','How much an option price changes for a $1 move in the underlying stock.'],
['Disposition effect','The bias to sell winners too soon and hold losers too long.'],
['Doji','A candle with nearly equal open and close, showing indecision.'],
['Drawdown','The decline from a peak in account value to a subsequent low.'],
['EMA','Exponential moving average; an average that weights recent prices more.'],
['Edge','A repeatable situation with positive expectancy after costs.'],
['ETF','Exchange-traded fund; a basket of securities that trades like a stock.'],
['Expectancy','The average result per trade: win rate x average win minus loss rate x average loss minus costs.'],
['Expiration','The date an option or futures contract ends.'],
['False breakout','A break of a level that quickly reverses, trapping breakout traders.'],
['FINRA','Financial Industry Regulatory Authority; the self-regulatory body for U.S. broker-dealers.'],
['Float','The number of shares available to trade publicly.'],
['FOMC','Federal Open Market Committee, which sets U.S. interest-rate policy.'],
['FOMO','Fear of missing out; a driver of chasing trades.'],
['Futures contract','A standardized agreement to buy or sell an asset at a set price on a future date.'],
['Gap','A stock opening far from its prior close, usually on overnight news.'],
['Good faith violation','A cash account violation from buying with unsettled funds and selling before they settle.'],
['Halt','A pause in trading of a stock, often for news or volatility.'],
['Implied volatility (IV)','The market\'s expectation of future movement, embedded in option prices.'],
['Intraday margin','The 2026 FINRA framework requiring firms to monitor equity against actual positions during the trading day.'],
['Leverage','Using borrowed money or derivatives to control a larger position than your cash allows.'],
['Limit order','An order to buy or sell at a specified price or better.'],
['Liquidity','How easily an asset can be traded without moving the price.'],
['Long','Owning a security, profiting if the price rises.'],
['MAE / MFE','Maximum adverse / favorable excursion: how far a trade moved against / for you before it ended.'],
['Margin','Borrowed money from your broker; also the collateral required for leveraged positions.'],
['Margin call','A demand to add funds when account equity falls below requirements; positions may be liquidated.'],
['Market cap','Share price multiplied by shares outstanding.'],
['Market order','An order to trade immediately at the best available price.'],
['Mark-to-market','Accounting that treats open positions as sold at year-end; the Section 475(f) election for qualifying traders.'],
['NBBO','National best bid and offer across venues.'],
['NFA','National Futures Association, the self-regulator for U.S. futures and forex firms.'],
['Open interest','The number of outstanding option or futures contracts.'],
['Opening range','The high and low of the first minutes (often 5, 15 or 30) of the session.'],
['Overfitting','Tuning rules until they fit historical noise, causing failure on new data.'],
['Paper trading','Simulated trading with fake money.'],
['Pattern day trader (PDT) rule','The former FINRA rule requiring $25,000 equity after four day trades in five days; replaced by intraday margin in 2026.'],
['Payment for order flow','Payments from wholesalers to brokers for routing retail orders to them.'],
['Portfolio heat','Total risk across all open positions.'],
['Position size','The number of shares or contracts in a trade.'],
['Premium','The price paid for an option, quoted per share.'],
['Profit factor','Gross profits divided by gross losses.'],
['Pump and dump','A scheme that hypes a thinly traded stock so promoters can sell to buyers.'],
['Put option','A contract giving the buyer the right to sell a security at the strike price by expiration.'],
['R multiple','A trade\'s result measured in units of initial risk.'],
['Relative volume','Today\'s volume compared with normal volume for that time of day.'],
['Resistance','A price area where selling has repeatedly halted advances.'],
['RSI','Relative strength index; a 0 to 100 gauge of recent price momentum.'],
['Scalping','Very short-term trading for tiny profits, highly sensitive to costs.'],
['Scanner','A tool that filters the market for stocks meeting criteria.'],
['SEC','U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission.'],
['Short selling','Selling borrowed shares, hoping to buy them back cheaper.'],
['Short squeeze','A rapid rise that forces short sellers to cover, pushing price higher.'],
['SIPC','Securities Investor Protection Corporation; protects customer assets if a broker fails, not against market losses.'],
['Slippage','The difference between expected and actual execution price.'],
['SPIVA','S&P scorecards comparing active funds to their benchmarks.'],
['Spread','The gap between bid and ask.'],
['Stop-limit order','A stop that becomes a limit order once triggered; may not fill.'],
['Stop-loss','An order that exits a position at a set price to limit loss.'],
['Strike price','The price at which an option can be exercised.'],
['Support','A price area where buying has repeatedly halted declines.'],
['Swing trading','Holding positions for days to weeks.'],
['T+1','Trades settle one business day after execution.'],
['Theta','The rate at which an option loses value as time passes.'],
['Tick','The minimum price increment of an instrument.'],
['Tilt','An emotional state in which decision quality drops.'],
['Time in force','How long an order stays active (day, GTC, IOC, FOK).'],
['Trader tax status','An IRS category for people trading as a business, with specific tests.'],
['VIX','An index of expected S&P 500 volatility, the "fear gauge."'],
['Volatility','How much and how fast price moves.'],
['Volume','Number of shares or contracts traded in a period.'],
['VWAP','Volume-weighted average price for the day.'],
['Wash sale','Selling at a loss and rebuying the same or substantially identical security within 30 days, which disallows the loss for that year.'],
['0DTE','Options expiring the same day.']
];
